New preview switch `-preview=orderIndependence` makes `static if` and `mixin` results independent of evaluation order

`static if`, `static foreach` and `mixin` can add declarations to a scope while
the compiler is still analyzing it. Until now, a lookup done before such a
declaration was added could see a different answer than the same lookup done
after, so the meaning of a program could depend on the order in which the
compiler happened to evaluate declarations, including the order of files on the
command line.

With `-preview=orderIndependence`:

$(UL
$(LI A name declared by a `static if` or `static foreach` is found even when it
is used before the compiler would otherwise have evaluated that `static if`.
This fixes forward references such as
$(LINK2 https://github.com/dlang/dmd/issues/18151, issue 18151).)
$(LI If whether a name exists depends on the very lookup of that name, it is an error.)
$(LI A string mixin or mixin template that declares a name after a lookup has
already found it missing, adds an overload after overloads were resolved, or
adds a member after `__traits(allMembers)` listed the members, is an error.)
)

-------
static if (!is(typeof(foo))) void foo() {} // error: depends on itself

struct S
{
    Foo f;                       // OK now, was "undefined identifier"
}
static if (true) struct Foo {}
-------

Code that tests whether a template's own result is "already defined" to
short-circuit, such as `static if (!is(typeof(T) == bool)) enum T = ...;`, must
be rewritten, for example with recursion. `core.internal.traits.anySatisfy` and
`allSatisfy` have been rewritten this way.
